WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.801
Relationships decide how filters move between tables.

2
00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:08.045
On this report page there are two table visuals:

3
00:00:08.045 --> 00:00:12.760
Order IDs from Order Details, and Product Names from Products.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.040
In Model view, Products and Order Details are joined on Product ID.

5
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.140
It is many to one:

6
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.600
each product appears many times in Order Details, but only once in Products.

7
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.970
Back on the report, when I select a product name, the Order ID table

8
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.455
shows only the orders for that product.

9
00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:59.360
The filter flows from the one side to the many side.

10
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.438
With a Single direction, filters flow only one way.

11
00:01:19.438 --> 00:01:24.786
You can set the direction to Both, but use it carefully, because it can

12
00:01:24.786 --> 00:01:27.460
slow the model and give confusing results.

13
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.531
A large model can be hard to read.

14
00:01:52.531 --> 00:01:56.960
Click the plus sign at the bottom of Model view to add a new

15
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.440
layout, then drag in only the tables you want to study.

16
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.313
Here I add All Customers and North Customers.

17
00:02:28.313 --> 00:02:32.040
Customer ID appears more than once in both tables.

18
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.561
So Power BI sets the relationship to many to many and shows a warning.

19
00:02:45.561 --> 00:02:51.520
Many to many is allowed, but duplicated keys often mean the data needs cleaning first.

